    prerequisites: null,
    content: "# A Developer Toolkit for Nothing: GitHub, Copilot Free and Your First Codespace\n\n> **Stage:** idea awaiting full write-up. The detail below is the approved lab idea (June 2026 free-tier facts checked 12 June 2026). Full lab.md, voiceover and image prompts follow once this idea is picked.\n\n- **Type:** Explore\n- **Lesson(s):** L18 (AI Power Tools), L19 (Choosing Your Coding Tool)\n- **Tools and access:** GitHub free account (email only, no card, no phone). GitHub Copilot Free (2,000 completions plus 50 chat requests/month, confirmed on github.com/features/copilot/plans 12 June 2026). GitHub Codespaces free (120 core-hours/month). All in the browser; no installs at all.\n- **The task:** Students create a GitHub account, enable Copilot Free, create a repository from GitHub's \"hello world\" template, open it in a Codespace (VS Code in the browser), and ask Copilot Chat three things: \"explain what this repository is\", \"add a README section introducing me\", and \"what does a commit mean?\" They commit the README change with Copilot's guidance. The point is demystification: a full professional development environment, in a browser tab, for nothing.\n- **Timing:** 10 min GitHub account and Copilot Free enablement; 10 min create repo and launch Codespace; 25 min the three Copilot Chat tasks and the commit; 10 min write three sentences on what a repo, a commit and a Codespace are; 5 min save screenshots. Total 60.\n- **What the student learns:** The professional developer toolchain is free and browser-based, and AI chat inside it means nothing has to be memorised.\n- **Deliverable:** A GitHub repository URL showing their committed README edit.\n- **Difficulty:** Late M1.",
    instructions: [{ step: 1, title: "Lab Walkthrough", content: "# A Developer Toolkit for Nothing: GitHub, Copilot Free and Your First Codespace\n\n> **Stage:** idea awaiting full write-up. The detail below is the approved lab idea (June 2026 free-tier facts checked 12 June 2026). Full lab.md, voiceover and image prompts follow once this idea is picked.\n\n- **Type:** Explore\n- **Lesson(s):** L18 (AI Power Tools), L19 (Choosing Your Coding Tool)\n- **Tools and access:** GitHub free account (email only, no card, no phone). GitHub Copilot Free (2,000 completions plus 50 chat requests/month, confirmed on github.com/features/copilot/plans 12 June 2026). GitHub Codespaces free (120 core-hours/month). All in the browser; no installs at all.\n- **The task:** Students create a GitHub account, enable Copilot Free, create a repository from GitHub's \"hello world\" template, open it in a Codespace (VS Code in the browser), and ask Copilot Chat three things: \"explain what this repository is\", \"add a README section introducing me\", and \"what does a commit mean?\" They commit the README change with Copilot's guidance. The point is demystification: a full professional development environment, in a browser tab, for nothing.\n- **Timing:** 10 min GitHub account and Copilot Free enablement; 10 min create repo and launch Codespace; 25 min the three Copilot Chat tasks and the commit; 10 min write three sentences on what a repo, a commit and a Codespace are; 5 min save screenshots. Total 60.\n- **What the student learns:** The professional developer toolchain is free and browser-based, and AI chat inside it means nothing has to be memorised.\n- **Deliverable:** A GitHub repository URL showing their committed README edit.\n- **Difficulty:** Late M1." }],
