    prerequisites: null,
    content: "# Fix My Page: Copilot Free vs Gemini Code Assist Inside VS Code\n\n> **Stage:** idea awaiting full write-up. The detail below is the approved lab idea (June 2026 free-tier facts checked 12 June 2026). Full lab.md, voiceover and image prompts follow once this idea is picked.\n\n- **Type:** Bakeoff\n- **Lesson(s):** L19 (Choosing Your Coding Tool), L18 (AI Power Tools)\n- **Tools and access:** VS Code (free, user-level install, no admin needed) or a GitHub Codespace from Lab 22 to skip installing anything. GitHub Copilot Free (account from Lab 22). Gemini Code Assist for individuals (free, 1,000 requests/day, Google sign-in, confirmed on Google's quotas page 12 June 2026). Both are ordinary VS Code extensions.\n- **The task:** GWTH provides a small single-file web page with 3 planted bugs (a button that does nothing, a misspelled variable, text that overflows on mobile). Students open it with each assistant in turn and use only chat: \"this button does nothing, find out why and fix it.\" Same 3 bugs, both assistants, scored on whether the fix worked first time and how well the assistant explained the cause.\n- **Timing:** 12 min open Codespace (or VS Code) and install both extensions; 5 min download the broken page and brief; 30 min fix 3 bugs with each assistant (15 each); 9 min scoring sheet; 4 min save. Total 60.\n- **What the student learns:** AI assistants turn \"broken and mysterious\" into \"explained and fixed\", and explanation quality is what separates the tools.\n- **Deliverable:** The fixed page working in a browser plus the scoring sheet.\n- **Difficulty:** Late M1.",
    instructions: [{ step: 1, title: "Lab Walkthrough", content: "# Fix My Page: Copilot Free vs Gemini Code Assist Inside VS Code\n\n> **Stage:** idea awaiting full write-up. The detail below is the approved lab idea (June 2026 free-tier facts checked 12 June 2026). Full lab.md, voiceover and image prompts follow once this idea is picked.\n\n- **Type:** Bakeoff\n- **Lesson(s):** L19 (Choosing Your Coding Tool), L18 (AI Power Tools)\n- **Tools and access:** VS Code (free, user-level install, no admin needed) or a GitHub Codespace from Lab 22 to skip installing anything. GitHub Copilot Free (account from Lab 22). Gemini Code Assist for individuals (free, 1,000 requests/day, Google sign-in, confirmed on Google's quotas page 12 June 2026). Both are ordinary VS Code extensions.\n- **The task:** GWTH provides a small single-file web page with 3 planted bugs (a button that does nothing, a misspelled variable, text that overflows on mobile). Students open it with each assistant in turn and use only chat: \"this button does nothing, find out why and fix it.\" Same 3 bugs, both assistants, scored on whether the fix worked first time and how well the assistant explained the cause.\n- **Timing:** 12 min open Codespace (or VS Code) and install both extensions; 5 min download the broken page and brief; 30 min fix 3 bugs with each assistant (15 each); 9 min scoring sheet; 4 min save. Total 60.\n- **What the student learns:** AI assistants turn \"broken and mysterious\" into \"explained and fixed\", and explanation quality is what separates the tools.\n- **Deliverable:** The fixed page working in a browser plus the scoring sheet.\n- **Difficulty:** Late M1." }],
